 # Ray-primitive intersection
 
-Also called: Ray-object intersection, Ray-scene intersection, Ray-primitive test
+Also called: Ray-object intersection, Ray-scene intersection
 
 ## Overview
-Ray-primitive intersection determines whether a viewing ray intersects a geometric primitive (such as a triangle, sphere, or box) and computes the parametric distance $t$ to the intersection point. It solves the fundamental geometric query required for volume rendering traversal, occlusion culling, and surface reconstruction, serving as the atomic operation for all higher-level spatial acceleration structures. It is distinguished from sibling methods by its role as the generic interface for arbitrary geometry, often involving coordinate space transformations to local frames, whereas [Ray generation and camera models](770-ray-generation-and-camera-models.md) defines the rays and [Parallel and GPU primitives](771-parallel-and-gpu-primitives.md) handles the execution model.
+Ray-primitive intersection computes the geometric relationship between a parametric ray and a specific scene primitive (e.g., box, triangle, sphere, implicit surface) to determine if, where, and how they intersect [S1, S3, S3604]. It solves the fundamental problem of identifying the closest valid intersection point along a ray, which is required for shading, volume traversal bounds, and occlusion testing [S1, S3, S3604]. This family is distinguished from [Ray-box intersection](776-ray-box-intersection.md) and [Sphere Intersection](1620-sphere-intersection.md) by its role as the generic interface for arbitrary geometry, handling coordinate space transformations and delegating specific algebraic solutions to the object's internal representation rather than implementing the algebra directly in the traversal loop [S3604, unofficial].
 
