+### [Ray-Splat Intersection](6816-ray-splat-intersection.md)
+Ray-Splat Intersection intersects a viewing ray against a hierarchical splat mesh to determine visible Gaussian splats for rendering. It supports both exact ellipsoid intersection and approximate sphere intersection, enabling efficient culling of non-visible splats in 3D Gaussian Splatting pipelines. It differs from sibling methods by operating on a tree-structured collection of oriented ellipsoids/spheres rather than static primitives or voxel grids.
+
+### Medium Stack Update
+Updates the ray's medium stack when crossing a surface, pushing a new medium if entering and popping if exiting [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Check Transmission:** Determine if transmission occurred using $(wo \cdot n < 0) \neq (wi \cdot n < 0)$ [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Update Stack:** If entering ($wi \cdot n < 0$) and the primitive has a medium, push the medium to the ray; if exiting, pop the medium [S4, unofficial].
+
+### Ray Marching via C++ Extension
+Performs ray-sphere intersection or ray marching against the SDF grid using a C++ renderer extension to find the rough intersection position for each pixel [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Execution:** Initialize output tensors for intersection position, voxel index, and ray direction. Call the C++ ray matching function with camera parameters (eye position), grid bounds, resolution, and grid data [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Extraction:** Extract the rough intersection position, the index of the minimum corner of the intersecting voxel, and the ray direction from the outputs [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Pitfall:** Pixels with no intersection are marked by `voxel_min_point_index` being -1. These must be handled to avoid invalid memory access during interpolation [S5, unofficial].
+
+### Ray Occlusion Test
+Tests if rays intersect any geometry without returning hit details, used for simple occlusion checks [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Execution:** Initialize `RTCRay` or `RTCRay8`. Set origin, direction, `tnear = minDist`, `tfar = InfinityDist`. Init context. Call `rtcOccluded1` or `rtcOccluded8` [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Result:** Return `ray.tfar < 0.0f` [S2, unofficial].
+
+### Point-Plane Reflection
+Reflects a point $p$ across a plane defined by normal `planeNormal` and offset `offset`, modifying $p$ in place and returning the sign of the distance to the plane [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Formula:** $t = p \cdot n + c$ [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Step:** If $t < 0$, update $p \leftarrow p - 2tn$ [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Return:** `sign(t)` [S6, unofficial].
+
+### Clipping Cell-Level Slice to Active Rays
+An optimization to reduce cell primitives in a slice by intersecting individual rays with the world-space bounding box of the current $u,v$ slice [S7, unofficial].
+*   **Step:** Ignore rays that have already successfully hit a cell (inactive rays), even if they intersect the slice bounding box [S7, unofficial].
+
+### BVH-based UDF evaluation with analytic gradient
+Computes the unsigned distance from query points to a triangle mesh using a BVH, and provides an analytic gradient for backpropagation [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Forward:** Flatten input $x$ to $[N, 3]$. Query BVH for distance `udf`, triangle ID `tri_id`, and barycentric coordinates `uvw`. Clamp `uvw` to $[10^{-9}, 1-10^{-9}]$. Interpolate nearest point on triangle using `uvw` and triangle vertices. Return `udf` reshaped to input shape [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Backward:** Retrieve saved $x$ and `nearest_points`. Compute `diff = x - nearest_points`. Compute `norm = diff.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True) + 10^{-8}`. Gradient is `grad_output` scaled by `diff/norm` [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Formula:** $\nabla_x \text{UDF}(x) = \frac{x - p_{\text{nearest}}}{\|x - p_{\text{nearest}}\|}$ where $p_{\text{nearest}}$ is the closest point on the mesh [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Pitfall:** `uvw` is clamped to $[10^{-9}, 1-10^{-9}]$ to avoid invalid barycentric coordinates at triangle edges/corners [S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The classification of ray-object intersections into five classes based on intersection type, data type, and early termination capability was established in volumetric ray tracing literature [S3, unofficial]. Class 1 involves geometric data requiring a single intersection point, computed analytically. Classes 2-5 involve volumetric data, ranging from point intersections with early termination (Class 2) to segment intersections requiring all values for compositing (Class 5) [S3, unofficial]. The field has evolved to include specialized methods for modern rendering techniques, such as Gaussian Splatting [S1, S2, S5, S8, unofficial] and implicit surface rendering [S7, S8, unofficial], as well as hardware-accelerated approaches using OptiX [S1, unofficial] and OpenGL geometry shaders [S1, unofficial].
+The classification of ray-object intersections into five classes based on intersection type (point vs. segment), data type (geometric vs. volumetric), and early termination capability was formalized in volumetric ray tracing literature [S3, unofficial]. Class 1 covers geometric data intersections requiring a single point, while Classes 2-5 cover volumetric data intersections ranging from early-terminating point queries to full segment compositing [S3, unofficial]. The use of Plücker coordinates for ray-triangle intersection provides a robust alternative to barycentric methods by evaluating winding orders via cross products [S1, 909, unofficial]. Modern implementations often leverage hardware-accelerated ray tracing (e.g., OptiX, Embree) for these primitives, handling instance IDs and normal orientations with platform-specific considerations [S2, unofficial].
 
